# lowered from the probe after.json by the fixture lowering (private tooling)
# The lane runs `ds-formal-tests --fixture` on this and diffs against
# after.expected (scripts/formal/check_idris.py). The Idris model's temporal
# fragment: an `after` clause lowered as the
# compiler lowers it (LTAC.3.1) on Rete/NetworkTimer.idr's reading. The
# timer kind 1, `$Timer_arm_0`, is synthetic (LTAC.3.2, RFCS.6a.5): the
# runner prints neither its facts nor their ids. Rule 0 `arm` logs the
# Trig's key and emits the timer with that key captured as its payload
# (LTAC.3.3, LTAC.3.5: an `emitx` of the body's one read, `slot 0 key 0`);
# rule 1 `$arm_after_0` (LTAC.8.1) is the follow-up: the timer's single
# positive pattern under `dwell 0 >= 5 s` -- the timer's entry stamp being
# the sample the parent's firing held, `monotonic_ns() >= fire_at` is the
# `dwell` gate (LTAC.3.4, LTIM.2.8.6) -- the body's log with the read
# rewritten to the timer's payload slot (`slot 0 pay 0`), then `withdraw 0`
# (LTAC.4.2). The time map's unit is 3 s, the gcd of the two advances, so
# the ticks are 1 and 2 and `dwell` is scaled by 3000000000 against the
# delay in nanoseconds. Two Trigs (keys 1 and 2, the key codes of one Int)
# asserted before one pump: `arm` fires twice and arms two timers stamped
# 0; at 3 s nothing is due; at 6 s both are, and the follow-up fires twice,
# the earlier-minted timer first (LTAC.5.4, LDET.5.5), each firing logging
# the captured key and retracting its timer.
schema 0 relation Trig
alpha 0 0
schema 1 explicit $Timer_arm_0
alpha 1 1
synthetic 1
rule 0 arm
pattern 0 0 0
log 0 0 1 slot 0 key 0
emitx 0 1 0 1 slot 0 key 0
rule 1 $arm_after_0
pattern 1 1 0
where 1 0 ge mul lit i:3000000000 dwell 0 lit i:5000000000
log 1 0 1 slot 0 pay 0
withdraw 1 0
step assert _0 0 1000000000000000000018 1
step assert _1 0 1000000000000000000034 1
step pump
step tick 1
step pump
step tick 2
step pump
